/* Track: Being disagreed with. Ordered foundational first.
 *
 * The half of the subject that people skip, and the half with more leverage.
 * Everything in the other eight tracks is an individual working against a
 * price. This track is about setting the price, which is a much cheaper way
 * to change the amount of dissent you get. */
(window.LESSONS = window.LESSONS || []).push(
{
  id: "receiving-your-reaction-sets-the-price",
  track: "receiving", level: "everyday",
  title: "What you do in the ten seconds after an objection sets the price for everyone watching",
  source: "Practice in team leadership, consistent with the psychological safety literature on how local leader behaviour shapes voice",
  gateIntro: "Someone on your team has just contradicted you in a meeting with nine people in it. The contradiction is partly wrong, delivered slightly clumsily, and there is a correct and mildly embarrassing reply available to you. Everyone is watching. Whatever you do next takes about ten seconds and will be remembered by the other eight for months.",
  gatePrompt: "You have ten seconds. Write exactly what you say.",
  evidence: "No trial isolating the ten seconds. The psychological safety literature consistently finds that local leader behaviour is among the strongest correlates of whether team members speak up, more so than organisational policy, and that variation in safety is largely within organisations rather than between them. That body of work is predominantly cross-sectional and self-reported, so it establishes association rather than the causal claim this entry makes.",
  replication: "craft",
  idea: "Treat your response to a public objection as a broadcast to everyone present about what objecting costs, and answer the strongest version of it before correcting any part of it.",
  why: "You are having two conversations at once and only one of them is with the person who spoke. The second one is with the eight people watching, and what they are extracting is a price. If the objection is met with a correct, efficient, mildly embarrassing reply, the price they observe is embarrassment for an imperfect challenge, and the rational response is to challenge only when certain. Since certainty arrives late, that setting produces a team that objects after decisions rather than before them.\n\nThe move that costs you almost nothing is to take the strongest version first. You can correct the wrong part afterwards and it lands completely differently, because you have already demonstrated that the challenge was worth having. Doing it in the other order, correction first, means everything after the correction is heard as consolation. This is also why the ten seconds matter more than anything you say later in a one-to-one: the eight people watching are not in the one-to-one, and their update has already happened.",
  failureMode: "You give the correct reply. It is fair, accurate and slightly deflating. Nobody is upset and nothing looks wrong. Over the following two months, objections in that meeting decline, and you attribute it to the plan being better understood rather than to a price you set in ten seconds.",
  worked: "Strongest version first, correction second, and make the value explicit:\n\n\"Hold on, that is worth taking seriously. The part I think is right is that we have no way to detect the failure if it happens overnight, and I had not thought about that. The part I would push back on is the volume estimate, which I think is out by a factor of ten, but that does not change the detection point.\n\nSo I want to keep the detection question open rather than close this now. Good catch.\"\n\nGood catch is not decoration. It is the price signal, said out loud, in front of the eight people who are calibrating on it.",
  experiment: "Ask a colleague to watch you for a month and note what you do in the first ten seconds after any challenge. You cannot observe this yourself. What people usually learn is that they have a consistent reflex, and that they had no idea what it was.",
  reflection: "What is your reflex in the first ten seconds after being contradicted, and who could tell you?",
  recall: {
    q: "Why do the ten seconds after a public objection matter more than a later private conversation, and what is the ordering rule?",
    a: "Because you are broadcasting a price to everyone watching, and their update happens immediately and is not available for revision in a one-to-one they are not in. The rule is to answer the strongest version of the objection first and correct the wrong parts afterwards, since correction first makes everything following it sound like consolation."
  },
  deepDive: "Someone challenged me in a meeting and I want to check my response. Here is what I said. Tell me what the room learned from it."
},
{
  id: "receiving-the-first-uncomfortable-one",
  track: "receiving", level: "costly",
  title: "Your invitation to disagree is worth nothing until the first uncomfortable one arrives",
  source: "Practice in team leadership, and the general observation that stated openness is tested rather than believed",
  gateIntro: "You have told your team three times, sincerely, that you want to be challenged and that nobody should agree with you to be polite. Nothing has changed. This morning someone told you, in a meeting, that the process you designed and are proud of is generating more work than it saves and that everybody has been working around it. They are probably right.",
  gatePrompt: "This is the test and you have one shot. Write what you say.",
  evidence: "No trial. The claim that stated invitations to disagree are discounted until tested is practitioner observation, consistent with the finding in the voice literature that observed managerial behaviour predicts speaking up better than stated policy does. There is no measurement of a single-event effect, and the framing of one decisive moment is a simplification of what is probably a gradual calibration.",
  replication: "craft",
  idea: "Understand that your team is waiting for evidence rather than assurance, and that the first genuinely unwelcome challenge is the entire test of everything you have said.",
  why: "Assurances about openness are cheap to give and everybody knows it, so they are treated as an intention rather than as information. What people are actually waiting for is a case: a moment where somebody said something the leader did not want to hear, and the leader's reaction was observable. Until that has happened, three sincere statements and none are equivalent, which is why nothing has changed after your three.\n\nThis moment is unusually high stakes and it is a good stake to have, because it is one of the few interventions available that is genuinely decisive rather than incremental. The specific trap is that the challenge is about something you built and are proud of, which is the version of the test you would not have chosen and the only version that counts. An easy challenge, met well, proves nothing, because meeting an easy challenge well is what everybody does. What is being observed is whether the invitation holds when it costs you something visible.",
  failureMode: "You respond with a defence of the process that is measured, reasonable, and contains three good points. Nothing about it is unfair. The team's conclusion is that the invitation applies to things you have no stake in, which is the accurate reading, and the invitation is not tested again.",
  worked: "Concede fast, concede specifically, and act on it in a way that is visible:\n\n\"That is uncomfortable and I think you are right. I built that process, so I am the last person who was going to notice people were routing around it.\n\nTell me the two worst parts. I would rather cut it than defend it, and if it is generating more work than it saves I want it gone this week rather than improved next quarter.\n\nAnd say this in the meeting rather than to me afterwards next time as well. This is exactly the thing I have been asking for and I want everyone to have seen what happens.\"\n\nThe last paragraph is the broadcast. The room needs to be told explicitly that this was the thing, or half of them will read it as a one-off about a process that happened to be bad.",
  experiment: "You cannot run this one, you can only be ready for it. What you can do is decide in advance what your response will be, because in the moment you will produce your reflex, and the reflex is usually a defence delivered so reasonably that you will not notice it was one.",
  reflection: "Has anyone told you something genuinely unwelcome in the last quarter, and what did you do in the first thirty seconds?",
  recall: {
    q: "Why do repeated invitations to challenge change nothing, and what makes the decisive moment decisive?",
    a: "Because assurances are cheap and known to be, so they read as intention rather than information, and the team is waiting for an observed case instead. The decisive moment is a challenge to something you built and are invested in, because meeting an easy challenge well proves nothing; what is being tested is whether the invitation holds when honouring it costs you something visible."
  },
  deepDive: "Someone challenged something I built and am proud of. Help me draft a response that concedes fast and acts visibly."
},
{
  id: "receiving-do-not-explain-immediately",
  track: "receiving", level: "everyday",
  title: "The reflex to explain why you did it closes the channel before the objection is finished",
  source: "Interviewing and coaching practice, where premature explanation is treated as the main failure of the listener",
  gateIntro: "Somebody has started telling you why the on-call rotation is not working. Four sentences in, you already know the answer, because the constraint that produced the rotation is one they do not know about. Your explanation is accurate and would take twenty seconds. They are still talking and you have already begun composing it.",
  gatePrompt: "You have the answer and they have not finished. Write what you say when they stop.",
  evidence: "No trial. Coaching and investigative interviewing both treat premature explanation as the primary listener failure, on the reasoning that it terminates disclosure and that what arrives after the first four sentences is usually the part the speaker was working up to. The claim is universal in that practice and has not been measured in a workplace setting.",
  replication: "craft",
  idea: "Hold your explanation until they have finished and you have asked whether there is more, because the useful part of an objection is usually behind the part you already have an answer to.",
  why: "Two things go wrong when you explain at sentence four. The obvious one is that you never hear sentences five through twelve, which is where people put the thing they were building up to, because most people open with the easiest version of their complaint and get to the real one once it seems to be going acceptably. Explaining early terminates that sequence at the safest point.\n\nThe less obvious one is what your explanation does to the exchange. It converts a report into a debate, and the person now has to either accept your answer or argue with it, when what they came to do was tell you something. Once it is a debate, they will accept your answer, because you have more context and more standing, and both of you will leave believing the matter was resolved by information. It was resolved by an interruption. The specific discipline is asking what else before you say anything, and it is unnatural enough that most people have to do it deliberately for months.",
  failureMode: "You explain the constraint at sentence four. It is a good explanation and they accept it. They never mention the second problem, which was the one about the handover gap, because the conversation moved on and reopening it would have meant starting again. You believe you handled an on-call complaint well.",
  worked: "Take everything first, ask for the rest, and only then explain:\n\n\"Keep going, and then tell me what else, because I suspect the rotation is not the only thing.\"\n\nThen, after they have finished:\n\n\"Right. There is one thing you might not know, which is that the two-timezone split was a customer contract rather than our choice. That does not answer the handover gap you raised, which I think is a real problem and separate. Can we take the handover one first?\"\n\nExplaining second, and explicitly naming what your explanation does not cover, is what stops the context landing as a rebuttal of everything they said.",
  experiment: "For a fortnight, in every conversation where someone raises a problem, ask what else before you say anything substantive, and ask it twice. Count how often the second or third item is the important one. For most people it is the majority, which is unsettling.",
  reflection: "When did you last explain a constraint before someone had finished, and what did they not go on to say?",
  recall: {
    q: "What two things go wrong when you explain at sentence four, and what is the discipline?",
    a: "You never hear the later part, where people put the concern they were working up to after opening with the safest version, and you convert a report into a debate the other person will concede because you have more context and standing, which leaves both of you thinking information resolved it. The discipline is asking what else, twice, before saying anything substantive."
  },
  deepDive: "Someone raised a complaint and I answered it immediately. Help me construct the follow-up that reopens what I cut off."
},
{
  id: "receiving-the-sandwich-is-overclaimed",
  track: "receiving", level: "costly",
  title: "The praise sandwich is taught everywhere and rests on nothing",
  source: "The compliment sandwich as taught in management training, and Kluger and DeNisi's meta-analysis of feedback interventions (Psychological Bulletin, 1996)",
  gateIntro: "You have to tell a senior engineer that their design review comments are driving two people off the team. Your training taught you to open with something positive, deliver the message, and close with something positive. You are drafting it that way and you have noticed that the opening praise is generic, because you could not think of a specific one that fits.",
  gatePrompt: "Write the opening without any praise in it, and without it being brutal.",
  evidence: "The sandwich has no supporting trials. It is a training convention repeated widely enough to function as established technique. The nearest relevant evidence is Kluger and DeNisi's meta-analysis of over six hundred effect sizes, which found feedback interventions improved performance on average but reduced it in over a third of cases, with the damage concentrated where attention was drawn to the self rather than the task. That does not test the sandwich; it undermines the assumption that feedback delivered kindly is safe.",
  replication: "overclaimed",
  idea: "Deliver the message without wrapping it in praise, because generic praise adjacent to criticism is decoded as preamble and it teaches people that your compliments predict bad news.",
  why: "The sandwich has two failure modes and they compound. The first is immediate: the recipient recognises the structure, because everyone has been taught it, and the opening praise is therefore processed as a countdown rather than as information. That is why your generic opener was hard to write. There was no specific praise available, and the reason you wanted one anyway was structural rather than communicative.\n\nThe second failure is slower and worse. If praise reliably precedes criticism, then praise becomes a signal for criticism, and you have degraded your ability to give either. People start listening past your compliments for what is coming, which means genuine praise stops functioning. Kluger and DeNisi do not test the sandwich, and what they do show is relevant: feedback frequently makes performance worse, and it does so most when attention is directed at the person. A structure that opens by evaluating the person, in either direction, is doing exactly that before the substance arrives.",
  failureMode: "You use the sandwich. The engineer hears a generic compliment, waits, receives the message, hears the closing compliment, and takes away that they were criticised and given some padding. The specific behaviour you needed to change is the middle of a structure they have decoded, and next month your genuine praise for their incident work is heard as a preamble to something.",
  worked: "State the subject, the observation, and the ask, with nothing wrapped around it:\n\n\"I want to talk about the review comments, and I am going to be direct rather than build up to it.\n\nTwo people have told me they are considering leaving, and both named design review. The specific thing both mentioned was comments that read as questioning whether they should be on the team rather than questioning the code. I have read back through the last month and I can see what they mean.\n\nWhat I want is for review comments to be about the change and not about the person writing it. I think that is a small change in wording and a big change in effect. Can we go through three examples together?\"\n\nPraise is not forbidden, it is unbundled. If this person is excellent, say so on a different day for its own reasons, where it will be believed.",
  experiment: "Look at the last three pieces of critical feedback you gave and check whether the opening praise was specific or generic. Generic openers are the tell that the praise was structural, and the recipient could tell too.",
  reflection: "When did you last open with praise you had to reach for, and what did the recipient think it was for?",
  recall: {
    q: "What are the two failure modes of the praise sandwich, and what does the feedback meta-analysis contribute?",
    a: "The structure is recognised, so opening praise is decoded as a countdown rather than information, and over time praise becomes a reliable signal for criticism, which destroys your ability to give either. The meta-analysis does not test the sandwich but shows feedback reduced performance in over a third of cases, worst when attention was directed at the person, which is what an evaluative opener does."
  },
  deepDive: "I have difficult feedback to deliver. Write the opening without any praise wrapper, and tell me where genuine praise should go instead."
},
{
  id: "receiving-ask-for-the-strongest-version",
  track: "receiving", level: "costly",
  title: "Ask for the strongest version of the objection, and mean it",
  source: "Practice in advisory and review settings, and the general convention that a critique should be tested against its best form",
  gateIntro: "A colleague has raised a mild objection to your proposal. You can tell from how carefully they phrased it that this is the diplomatic version, and that there is a stronger one they decided not to say. Answering the mild version would be easy and would close the topic. They are waiting for your response.",
  gatePrompt: "Write the sentence that asks for the version they did not say.",
  evidence: "No trial. This is convention in advisory practice and in serious review, and it rests on the same reasoning as steelmanning: an argument tested against a weakened version of the opposition has not been tested. Whether asking produces the stronger version has not been measured, and it visibly fails when the asker has a history of punishing what they receive.",
  replication: "craft",
  cheat: "\"That is the polite version. What is the strong one?\" Only ask if you will handle the answer well, because you get one attempt.",
  idea: "Ask explicitly for the harder version of the objection, because you were given the diplomatic one and answering it leaves the real one intact and unaddressed.",
  why: "Answering the mild version is a trap that feels like a success. It closes the topic, it is easy, and it leaves your proposal untested against the objection that actually exists, which is still in the other person's head and will be in the room again later when it is more expensive. You are also, without meaning to, confirming that the diplomatic version was the right choice, which means you will keep getting diplomatic versions.\n\nThe ask is short and it has one hard precondition: you have to handle the answer well, because you have invited it. Asking for the strong version and then reacting badly to it is worse than not asking, since it converts a general uncertainty about whether you want challenge into a specific piece of evidence that you do not. This is why the move should be used deliberately rather than as a habit. Use it when you actually want the objection, which is most often on decisions that are expensive to reverse, and be prepared for the answer to be something you did not want to hear, because that is what you asked for.",
  failureMode: "You answer the mild version well. The topic closes, your colleague concludes that the diplomatic register was correctly calibrated, and the real objection stays unsaid until it appears in a retrospective as something several people had been thinking.",
  worked: "Name what you noticed, ask for the strong version, and pre-commit to how you will take it:\n\n\"I think that is the diplomatic version. What is the one you decided not to say?\n\nI would rather have it now than in three months, and I am not going to argue with it in this meeting. I will take it away and come back to you, so you do not have to defend it on the spot.\"\n\nPromising not to argue with it immediately is what makes the strong version safe to give. It removes the thing people are actually avoiding, which is not your disagreement but having to defend a hard objection live in front of others.",
  experiment: "Use it once, on a decision that matters, and watch what you do with the answer. The point of the experiment is not the objection you receive, it is finding out whether you can take a strong one without your face doing something.",
  reflection: "When did somebody last give you the diplomatic version, and did you notice at the time?",
  recall: {
    q: "Why is answering the diplomatic version a trap, and what precondition does asking for the strong version carry?",
    a: "It closes the topic while leaving the real objection intact and unraised, and it confirms that the diplomatic register was correctly chosen, so you keep receiving it. The precondition is that you must handle the answer well: asking and then reacting badly converts general uncertainty about whether you want challenge into specific evidence that you do not."
  },
  deepDive: "A colleague gave me a soft objection and I think there is a harder one. Write the ask, including how I promise to handle it."
},
{
  id: "receiving-most-of-it-is-the-rater",
  track: "receiving", level: "costly",
  title: "A large part of what you are hearing is about the person saying it",
  source: "Scullen, Mount and Goff on the latent structure of performance ratings (Journal of Applied Psychology, 2000), and the wider multi-rater literature",
  gateIntro: "Three people have given you feedback on the same document. One thinks it is too long, one thinks it lacks detail, and one thinks the structure is wrong. You have spent the morning trying to reconcile all three into a revision and the revision is getting worse. All three of them are competent and none of them is being unhelpful.",
  gatePrompt: "Write the message you send to one of the three, asking the question that would break the tie.",
  evidence: "Analyses of multi-source performance ratings consistently find that idiosyncratic rater effects account for a substantially larger share of variance than the ratee's actual performance, with rater-specific variance often exceeding half and general performance accounting for a much smaller portion. This has been reproduced across studies and rating sources. It is a finding about performance appraisal specifically, and extending it to document feedback is an extrapolation, though a natural one.",
  replication: "replicated",
  idea: "Treat conflicting feedback as information about the raters rather than as a puzzle about the work, and resolve it by asking what each of them needed the thing to do.",
  why: "The multi-rater finding is uncomfortable and robust: when several people rate the same person, most of the variance is attributable to the rater rather than to the person being rated. Different people are systematically different in what they notice and how they weight it, and those differences swamp the signal about the target. Applied to your three reviewers, the reasonable conclusion is not that the document is simultaneously too long, insufficiently detailed and badly structured. It is that you have three readers with different needs, and averaging them produces something that serves none.\n\nThe practical move follows directly. Stop trying to reconcile the judgements and go back for the underlying need, because the need is the part that is about the document and the judgement is largely the part that is about the reader. Too long from someone who wanted a decision and insufficient detail from someone who has to implement it are not in conflict at all once you know that. They are a specification for two documents, or for one document with a summary, and neither of those is discoverable from the feedback itself.",
  failureMode: "You reconcile all three. The revision is longer to satisfy the detail request, restructured to satisfy the structure request, and now much worse for the reader who thought it was too long. All three reviewers are mildly dissatisfied with the second draft and none of them can say why.",
  worked: "Ask about use rather than about the feedback, which is the question that has an answer:\n\n\"Before I revise this, can I ask what you needed it for? I have three sets of feedback that point in different directions, which usually means you all wanted different things from it rather than that any of you is wrong.\n\nSpecifically: were you reading it to make a decision, to implement it, or to check it? I think that determines what should change, and I would rather serve one of those properly than average the three.\"\n\nNaming the three uses is what makes this fast to answer, and it usually produces an immediate yes, the first one, which is all you needed.",
  experiment: "Next time you get conflicting feedback, do not revise anything. Ask each reviewer what they were using it for and write the answers down. Most of the time the conflict dissolves entirely and you discover you were writing one document for three audiences.",
  reflection: "What have you revised into mediocrity by averaging feedback from people who wanted different things?",
  recall: {
    q: "What does the multi-rater variance finding show, and how should conflicting feedback be resolved?",
    a: "Idiosyncratic rater effects account for substantially more variance in ratings than the actual performance of the person rated, so different reviewers differ systematically in what they notice and weight. Resolve it by going back for each reviewer's underlying use rather than reconciling their judgements, since the use is the part that is about the work and averaging serves nobody."
  },
  deepDive: "I have three conflicting sets of feedback on one document. Help me work out what each reviewer actually needed rather than averaging them."
},
{
  id: "receiving-defensiveness-is-invisible",
  track: "receiving", level: "costly",
  title: "You cannot feel yourself being defensive, so arrange for somebody to tell you",
  source: "Practice in coaching and facilitation, where an agreed external signal is used because self-monitoring of defensiveness is unreliable",
  gateIntro: "A colleague has told you, carefully, that you get defensive when your architecture decisions are questioned. You do not experience yourself as defensive. You experience yourself as explaining context that people do not have, which is accurate, because you do have context they lack. You cannot find the behaviour they are describing from the inside.",
  gatePrompt: "Write what you ask them for, given that you cannot verify the thing they are describing.",
  evidence: "No trial. Coaching practice uses agreed external signals precisely because the internal experience of defending is indistinguishable from the internal experience of explaining, so self-report is uninformative. This is a working assumption of the profession rather than a measured finding, and it is consistent with the broader literature on the limits of introspective access.",
  replication: "craft",
  cheat: "Agree a signal with one person. \"Tell me when I am explaining rather than listening\" is the only reliable detector you have.",
  idea: "Set up an agreed external signal with one person, because the inside of defensiveness feels identical to the inside of explaining and you have no access to the difference.",
  why: "The reason you cannot find it is not denial. Defending and explaining produce the same internal experience: you have information the other person lacks, supplying it feels helpful and accurate, and it usually is accurate. What distinguishes them is not the content but the function, which is whether the information is being offered to inform or to close, and function is not visible from the inside. So your colleague's report and your self-observation are both honest and they cannot be reconciled by you thinking harder.\n\nWhat can be reconciled is behaviour, observed in the moment by somebody else. The value of an agreed signal is that it is specific, cheap and immediate, and that it removes the need for anybody to have a conversation about your character. A colleague who has agreed to say I think you are explaining rather than listening is giving you a datum at the moment it exists, which is the only time it is checkable. The alternative is a quarterly conversation about a pattern you cannot verify, which is what you are currently having.",
  failureMode: "You accept the feedback intellectually and cannot act on it, because you have nothing to act on. Six months later the same feedback arrives from a second person, and by then you have a pattern you still cannot see and a growing suspicion that everyone is being unfair, which is the reading that requires the least revision.",
  worked: "Ask for the signal rather than for more feedback, and make it easy to give:\n\n\"I believe you and I genuinely cannot see it, which I think is how this works rather than me being stubborn about it.\n\nSo can I ask for something specific. When it happens, say so at the time, in the meeting, using whatever words you like. Something like: you are explaining rather than listening. I will not argue with it in the moment, I will just stop talking. If you say it and I was actually right to explain, we can work that out afterwards.\n\nI would rather have three awkward interruptions than another six months of not being able to find this.\"\n\nCommitting to not arguing in the moment is what makes the signal usable. Without it, the first time they use it turns into the argument the signal was meant to prevent.",
  experiment: "Agree the signal with one person for a month and count how often it fires. The count is the finding. Most people expect one or two and are surprised, which is the whole point of using an external detector.",
  reflection: "Has anyone described a pattern in you that you cannot see, and did you arrange any way to observe it?",
  recall: {
    q: "Why is defensiveness undetectable by introspection, and what makes an agreed signal work?",
    a: "Because defending and explaining feel identical from the inside: both involve supplying information you genuinely have, and what differs is the function, whether the information informs or closes, which is not internally visible. A signal works because it produces a datum at the moment it exists, and it requires a commitment not to argue with it in the moment, which is what keeps it usable."
  },
  deepDive: "Someone told me I get defensive and I cannot see it. Help me design the signal and the commitment I make to whoever gives it."
},
{
  id: "receiving-do-not-tax-the-messenger",
  track: "receiving", level: "costly",
  title: "Great point, can you write that up is a tax on objecting",
  source: "Observation of meeting practice, and the general principle that assigning work as a response to input suppresses the input",
  gateIntro: "Somebody has raised a good objection about the observability gaps in the new service. Your instinct, which feels like taking it seriously, is to ask them to write up a proposal. They are already at capacity, the gap is not theirs to fix, and this is the third time this quarter you have responded to one of their observations this way.",
  gatePrompt: "Write the response that takes it seriously without assigning them anything.",
  evidence: "No trial. This is an observation about incentives rather than a finding: if raising an issue reliably results in owning it, the expected cost of raising rises with each instance and rational people raise fewer. It has not been measured, and the countervailing consideration is real, since the person who saw the problem often is the right owner and refusing to assign anything has its own failure mode.",
  replication: "craft",
  idea: "Separate acknowledging an objection from assigning its resolution, because reflexively handing the work to whoever raised it makes each observation cost the observer and they will stop making them.",
  why: "The response feels respectful and functions as a tax. From the raiser's side, the pattern is that noticing something costs you a project, and after three instances the calculation is explicit even if it is never articulated: mentioning the observability gap will cost me two weeks I do not have. The rational adaptation is to stop noticing out loud, and it is invisible to you because it produces no event.\n\nThe complication is that sometimes they are the right owner, and a rule against ever assigning would be worse. The test is whether you are assigning because they are best placed or because assigning is the fastest way to make the item stop being yours. That distinction is available to you if you check, and the honest check is whether you would have given this to them if somebody else had raised it. When the answer is no, the correct response is to acknowledge the objection, own the next step yourself, and come back to them, which costs you the thing you were trying to avoid and is the actual job.",
  failureMode: "You ask them to write it up. They do, at some cost, and the write-up sits unactioned because the gap was never yours to prioritise either. They have now learned that raising something costs two weeks and produces nothing, which is the strongest possible argument against raising the next one.",
  worked: "Acknowledge, take the next step yourself, and be explicit that you are not assigning it:\n\n\"That is a real gap and I am not going to ask you to write it up, because you are full and because it is not yours.\n\nI will take it to the platform planning session on Thursday and come back to you with either an owner or a reason it is not being done. Either way you will hear from me by Friday.\n\nIf it turns out you are the right person for it, I will ask you properly rather than assume, and you can say no.\"\n\nThe last line matters more than it looks. It converts a future assignment from an inevitability into a request, which is what makes raising the next thing cheap again.",
  experiment: "For a month, note every time you respond to an observation by assigning work to the observer. Then check whether you would have assigned it to them had somebody else raised it. The gap between those two numbers is the size of your tax.",
  reflection: "Who on your team has stopped raising things, and how many of their observations turned into work for them?",
  recall: {
    q: "Why does asking the raiser to write it up suppress future objections, and what is the honest test for when assigning is right?",
    a: "Because noticing something reliably costs the noticer a project, so the expected cost of raising rises and the rational adaptation is to stop, which produces no observable event. The test is whether you would have assigned it to them had somebody else raised it; if not, you are assigning to stop the item being yours, and the correct move is to own the next step and return with an answer."
  },
  deepDive: "Someone raised a good objection and my instinct is to ask them to own it. Help me decide whether that is right or whether I am offloading."
},
{
  id: "receiving-separate-hearing-from-agreeing",
  track: "receiving", level: "everyday",
  title: "Say whether you are agreeing or just listening, because they cannot tell",
  source: "Practice in decision communication, where acknowledgement and acceptance are distinguished explicitly",
  gateIntro: "Somebody has spent ten minutes making a case you have listened to carefully. You are not going to change the decision. You have nodded throughout, asked two good questions, and said that is a fair point twice. They are about to leave the room believing they have made progress, and they have not.",
  gatePrompt: "Write the closing sentence that makes clear what has and has not happened.",
  evidence: "No trial. Distinguishing acknowledgement from acceptance is standard in decision communication and negotiation practice, on the reasoning that attentive listening is routinely decoded as movement and that the resulting false expectation is more damaging than an immediate no. Nothing measures this, and it is possible that the ambiguity is sometimes useful, which is exactly why it persists.",
  replication: "craft",
  cheat: "\"I have heard you and I am not changing my mind\" is kinder than a nod that gets read as movement.",
  idea: "State explicitly whether listening has changed anything, because attentive listening is read as partial agreement and leaving that uncorrected is worse than saying no.",
  why: "Good listening behaviour and being persuaded look identical from the outside. Nods, good questions and that is a fair point are the signals of both, so the person leaves calibrating on evidence that means something different from what you intended. When the decision comes out unchanged, their honest reconstruction is that they were listened to as a formality, which is a harsher reading than the truth and one you created by not closing the loop.\n\nSaying it plainly is kinder than it sounds, and it is also more useful, because it lets them decide what to do next. Somebody who knows the decision has not moved can escalate, ask what would change it, or drop it, and all three of those are better than continuing to invest in a conversation that has already concluded. The version that damages relationships is not the clear no. It is the warm reception followed by an unchanged outcome, because that combination teaches people that your engagement carries no information.",
  failureMode: "They leave believing it went well. The decision is published unchanged the following week. They conclude that the meeting was a consultation exercise, which is what it will look like, and the next time they have something to raise they will weigh it against that experience.",
  worked: "Close the loop explicitly, distinguish the two things, and offer the route forward:\n\n\"I want to be straight about where that leaves us, because I have been nodding a lot and I do not want you leaving with the wrong impression.\n\nI have heard the argument and I understand it better than I did. It has not changed my decision. The reason is the external date, which is the thing I cannot move rather than the thing I disagree with you about.\n\nIf you want to take it further I will not treat that as going around me, and if you want to know what would change my mind, it is the date moving, which is Priya's call rather than mine.\"\n\nNaming what would change your mind and who owns it turns a dead end into a next step, which is the part that keeps the relationship intact.",
  experiment: "For a fortnight, end every conversation where you were persuaded of nothing with an explicit sentence saying so. Note how people react. The usual reaction is relief rather than disappointment, which is worth knowing.",
  reflection: "Who left a conversation with you recently believing they had moved you, when they had not?",
  recall: {
    q: "Why is a warm reception followed by an unchanged decision worse than a clear no?",
    a: "Because good listening and being persuaded look identical, so the person calibrates on evidence you did not intend, and when the outcome is unchanged their honest reconstruction is that the consultation was a formality. A clear no lets them escalate, ask what would change your mind, or drop it, and it preserves the information value of your engagement."
  },
  deepDive: "I listened to a long case and am not changing my decision. Write the closing that says so and still leaves them a route forward."
},
{
  id: "receiving-take-it-away",
  track: "receiving", level: "costly",
  title: "Answering a hard challenge immediately is how you commit to a position you have not thought about",
  source: "Practice in advisory and governance settings, where a response to a substantive challenge is deferred rather than improvised",
  gateIntro: "In a meeting with eleven people, someone has raised an objection to your plan that you have no immediate answer to. It is a good objection. There is an expectation in the room that you will respond now, and you can feel yourself constructing something that sounds adequate. Whatever you say in the next thirty seconds becomes your position.",
  gatePrompt: "Write what you say instead of an answer.",
  evidence: "No trial. Deferring a substantive response is convention in governance and advisory practice, on the reasoning that an improvised answer under observation becomes a public commitment which the commitment literature predicts will then be defended. The underlying commitment and consistency effects have reasonable support; the specific prescription has not been tested.",
  replication: "craft",
  cheat: "\"I do not have an answer to that. Let me take it away and come back Thursday.\" The improvised answer becomes a position you defend.",
  idea: "Say that you do not have an answer and name when you will, rather than improvising, because an improvised answer said in front of eleven people becomes a position you will then defend.",
  why: "The trap is not that your improvised answer will be wrong. It is that it becomes yours, publicly, in front of eleven witnesses, and everything the commitment literature says about publicly stated positions then applies to it. You will find yourself defending a sentence you constructed in thirty seconds under pressure, for months, against an objection that was good. That is a much worse outcome than the brief discomfort of having no answer.\n\nSaying you do not have one is also read far better than most people expect, provided it comes with a date. Without a date it sounds like deflection, and reasonably so. With one it reads as somebody who takes the objection seriously enough not to answer it badly, which is a stronger position than any improvisation available to you. The one thing that has to follow is that you come back on the date, including when the answer is that they were right, which is the case in which the temptation to let it lapse is strongest.",
  failureMode: "You improvise. It sounds adequate and it commits you. Two weeks later you have found the real answer, which is different from what you said, and changing your public position now costs more than the original objection did. You defend the improvisation instead, and it slowly becomes what you believe.",
  worked: "No answer, plus a date, plus what you will do:\n\n\"I do not have an answer to that, and I do not want to make one up in front of everyone because I will then be stuck with it.\n\nIt is a good objection and I think it might be right. Give me until Thursday. I will go through the retry path properly and come back with either an answer or a revision to the plan, and I will send it round to everyone here rather than just to you.\"\n\nCommitting to send it to the whole room is what stops this being a way of making the objection go quiet, and it is the difference between deferring and burying.",
  experiment: "The next time you have no answer, say so with a date instead of improvising. Then keep the date. The keeping is the entire experiment, because deferring is easy and returning is what makes it credible the second time.",
  reflection: "What position are you currently defending that you invented in thirty seconds under pressure?",
  recall: {
    q: "What is the real risk of improvising an answer to a hard challenge, and what makes deferral land well?",
    a: "Not that the answer is wrong but that it becomes a public position in front of witnesses, which you will then defend for months against a good objection, at a cost far exceeding the brief discomfort of having none. Deferral lands well when it carries a date and a commitment to return to the whole room, which distinguishes it from deflection and from burying the objection."
  },
  deepDive: "I was challenged in a meeting and improvised an answer I now think is wrong. Help me write the correction to the room."
},
{
  id: "receiving-close-the-loop",
  track: "receiving", level: "everyday",
  title: "Tell them what happened to their objection, especially when the answer is nothing",
  source: "Practice in suggestion and reporting schemes, where the reporting rate is known to depend on whether reporters learn the outcome",
  gateIntro: "Five weeks ago a junior engineer raised a concern about the deployment pipeline. You took it seriously, looked into it, and concluded it was a real issue that is not worth fixing this year given what else is queued. You made that decision three weeks ago. You have not told them, and they have not asked.",
  gatePrompt: "Write the message telling them their objection went nowhere.",
  evidence: "No trial. Incident reporting and suggestion schemes across aviation, healthcare and manufacturing treat feedback to the reporter as a design requirement, on the consistent operational finding that reporting rates fall when reporters do not learn what happened. The evidence is programme experience rather than controlled comparison, and the mechanism proposed, that silence is read as the report having been discarded, is inference.",
  replication: "craft",
  idea: "Report back to whoever raised something, including and especially when the outcome was that nothing will be done, because silence is indistinguishable from having ignored it.",
  why: "From where they are sitting, an objection raised and never mentioned again looks the same whether you investigated it thoroughly and made a considered trade-off or forgot about it in the car park. They have no way to tell those apart, and the second is the more common explanation in most organisations, so it is the reasonable inference. Your careful investigation, which nobody told them about, has produced exactly the same signal as not caring.\n\nThe case where this matters most is the one people avoid, which is when the answer is no. Telling somebody their concern was real and is not being addressed feels like it will discourage them, and the opposite is true: what discourages people is not learning that their objection lost, it is not learning anything. A no with a reason confirms the concern was taken seriously, tells them how decisions here get made, and leaves them able to raise it again when the trade-off changes. Silence teaches them that raising things is an act with no observable consequence, which is a much stronger argument against doing it again.",
  failureMode: "You never mention it. The engineer concludes their concern went nowhere, which is technically true and misses everything that happened in between. They do not raise the next one, and you never learn that there was a next one.",
  worked: "State the outcome, own the decision, give the reason and the condition that would change it:\n\n\"I owe you an answer on the pipeline thing you raised five weeks ago, and I am sorry it has taken this long.\n\nYou were right. The rollback path does not work the way the runbook says, and I confirmed it. We are not fixing it this year, because the platform team is fully committed to the migration and I could not make a case to pull anyone off it. That was my call and I think it is the wrong outcome, just the best one available.\n\nIf the migration slips or we have an incident that touches this, it goes back on the list. Raise it again if either happens, because I might miss it.\"\n\nYou were right, delivered before the no, is what stops the message reading as a rejection of the objection rather than of the timing.",
  experiment: "List everything anybody has raised to you in the last two months and mark which raisers you have gone back to. Close every open one this week, including the ones where the answer is nothing. The lateness is worth the awkwardness.",
  reflection: "Who raised something to you more than a month ago and has never been told what happened?",
  recall: {
    q: "Why is silence after an objection indistinguishable from ignoring it, and why does a no still work?",
    a: "Because the raiser cannot tell a considered trade-off from forgetting, and forgetting is the more common explanation in most organisations, so it is the reasonable inference, which means a careful investigation nobody was told about produces the same signal as indifference. A no with a reason confirms the concern was taken seriously, shows how decisions are made, and leaves the door open when conditions change."
  },
  deepDive: "Here are things people raised to me that I never went back on. Help me write the closing messages, including the ones where nothing happened."
},
{
  id: "receiving-openness-is-behaviour",
  track: "receiving", level: "costly",
  title: "Employees calibrate on what their own manager does, not on what the company says",
  source: "Detert and Burris, Leadership Behavior and Employee Voice (Academy of Management Journal, 2007)",
  gateIntro: "Your company has an open door policy, a values statement about candour, and an anonymous feedback tool. Your engagement survey shows people do not feel able to raise concerns. The proposed response from the leadership team is a communications campaign about the existing channels, and you are in the meeting where it is being agreed.",
  gatePrompt: "Write the intervention, addressed to a leadership team about to approve a campaign.",
  evidence: "In a multi-source field study of restaurant managers and their employees, managerial openness was more strongly associated with improvement-oriented voice than transformational leadership was, and the relationship ran through employees' sense of psychological safety. This is field survey data from one industry, cross-sectional, with voice partly self-reported, so it establishes association in one setting rather than a general causal law.",
  replication: "single",
  idea: "Treat voice as a property of individual managers rather than of the organisation, and measure it by team, because a company-wide campaign about channels does not touch the thing that determines whether people speak.",
  why: "The finding that matters here is where the variance lives. Openness of the immediate manager was the stronger predictor, and it operated through whether people felt it was safe, which means the relevant unit is the team rather than the company. A campaign about channels addresses the company level, where there is comparatively little variance to explain, and leaves the manager level untouched.\n\nThe consequence for your meeting is a specific alternative rather than just an objection. If voice varies by manager, then your survey data almost certainly varies by manager too, and looking at it that way is cheap and immediately actionable. Some teams will be fine and some will not, the pattern will point at particular people, and the intervention becomes coaching or accountability for a small number rather than a communications exercise for everyone. That is a less comfortable proposal, which is why the campaign is winning, and it is the one with a mechanism behind it.",
  failureMode: "The campaign runs. Awareness of the channels goes up and usage does not, because awareness was never the constraint. The following year's survey shows the same result, and the conclusion drawn is that people need more encouragement, which produces a second campaign.",
  worked: "Attack the level rather than the intent, and bring the cheaper alternative:\n\n\"I want to argue against the campaign, and not because I think the channels are bad. My concern is that we are intervening at the company level and I think almost all the variance is at the manager level.\n\nWhat I would like to do first is cut the survey data by team. If it is flat across teams, the campaign is right and I will support it. If it is concentrated, which is what I expect, then the problem is a small number of specific managers and a campaign will not touch it. That analysis takes a day and we already have the data.\"\n\nOffering to accept the campaign if the data is flat is what makes this a proposal rather than an objection, and it is a genuine offer because the analysis is cheap enough to run either way.",
  experiment: "Cut any existing voice or safety survey data by team and look at the spread. If it is wide, you have located the intervention, and you have also found out that your organisation has been treating a distribution as an average.",
  reflection: "Does your organisation measure whether people speak up at the company level or the team level, and what would the spread show?",
  recall: {
    q: "Where does the variance in employee voice sit, and what does that imply about organisation-wide interventions?",
    a: "Predominantly with the immediate manager, whose observed openness predicted improvement-oriented voice more strongly than broader leadership style and operated through employees' sense of safety. It implies that company-level campaigns address a level with little variance to explain, and that survey data should be cut by team, which usually reveals a distribution that has been reported as an average."
  },
  deepDive: "My company is planning a campaign about speaking up. Help me make the case for cutting the survey data by team first."
},
{
  id: "receiving-ask-those-who-owe-you-nothing",
  track: "receiving", level: "costly",
  title: "The most accurate feedback comes from people with the least to lose by giving it",
  source: "Practice in leadership assessment, and the standing observation that exit interviews produce information unavailable during employment",
  gateIntro: "You have asked your team for feedback repeatedly and received almost nothing useful. Meanwhile an engineer who left three months ago said more in one exit conversation than your whole team has said in a year, and a new joiner made two sharp observations in their first fortnight that nobody currently on the team has ever mentioned.",
  gatePrompt: "Write the request you send to somebody who has just resigned, or who joined last month.",
  evidence: "No trial. That exit interviews surface concerns not raised during employment is an old and consistent observation in human resources practice, and the explanation offered, that the cost of candour falls to near zero on departure, is straightforward. Nothing measures the accuracy of exit feedback relative to feedback given during employment, and there are obvious reasons it might be distorted in its own direction.",
  replication: "craft",
  idea: "Solicit deliberately from people whose cost of candour is low, meaning leavers, new joiners and people outside your reporting line, because your own team's silence is a report on the cost rather than on the content.",
  why: "Your team's lack of feedback is not an absence of observations. It is a price, and asking harder does not change a price. What changes is who you ask, and there are three groups whose price is structurally lower: people who are leaving and have no future to protect, people who have just arrived and can still see the things everybody else has normalised, and people outside your chain who bear no consequence.\n\nEach of these groups is a wasting asset, which is the part that makes this urgent rather than merely sensible. A leaver is available for a few weeks. A new joiner's outside eyes last about a month before they recalibrate. Neither of them will volunteer, because volunteering criticism as a departing or brand-new employee is socially odd, so both have to be asked directly. The honest caveat is that this feedback has its own distortions: leavers may be aggrieved and new joiners lack context. Both are still more informative than a silence you have already established you cannot break.",
  failureMode: "You keep asking your team, get nothing, and conclude either that things are fine or that people are disengaged. The leaver's observations go into an exit form that you never see, and the new joiner's fade within six weeks as they learn what is normal here.",
  worked: "Ask directly, name why you are asking them specifically, and make it clear there is nothing to lose:\n\n\"You are leaving in three weeks and you have nothing to gain from being polite to me, which is exactly why I am asking.\n\nWhat is the thing about how I run this team that people know and do not say to me? I am not looking for the diplomatic version and I am not going to argue with it or ask you to justify it. You will not have to work with me again either way.\n\nIf there is nothing, say nothing, and I will not push.\"\n\nFor a new joiner the ask is different because the asset is different:\n\n\"You have been here five weeks, which means you can still see things the rest of us have stopped noticing. What has struck you as odd that nobody has explained? I want the list before it stops seeming odd to you, because that happens fast.\"",
  experiment: "Ask the next person who leaves and the next person who joins, within their windows. Write down what they say without responding to it. Then check how much of it your current team could also have told you, which is usually most of it.",
  reflection: "Who has left in the last year that you never asked, and who joined recently whose window is still open?",
  recall: {
    q: "Why does asking your own team harder not work, and which three groups have a structurally lower cost of candour?",
    a: "Because the silence reflects a price rather than an absence of observations, and asking more insistently does not change the price. The three groups are leavers, who have no future to protect, new joiners, who can still see what everyone else has normalised, and people outside your reporting line. All three are wasting assets and none will volunteer, so each must be asked directly."
  },
  deepDive: "Someone on my team is leaving in three weeks. Write the request for honest feedback that makes it easy for them to give."
},
{
  id: "receiving-the-question-that-works",
  track: "receiving", level: "everyday",
  title: "Replace any concerns with a question that presupposes there is something",
  source: "Interviewing practice on presupposition in question design, and standard facilitation guidance on soliciting problems",
  gateIntro: "You have ended every review for six months with does anyone have any concerns. You have received concerns four times in six months, and in the same period three significant problems surfaced later that people knew about at the time. The question is clearly not working and you have kept asking it because you cannot think of a better one.",
  gatePrompt: "Write the replacement question. One sentence, and it should be hard to answer with no.",
  evidence: "No trial in this setting. Question design is well studied in survey methodology and investigative interviewing, where it is established that presupposition and framing materially change response rates and content, including the classic demonstrations that leading wording alters what people report. Applying that to soliciting objections in meetings is an extrapolation from a different domain.",
  replication: "craft",
  cheat: "Replace \"any concerns?\" with \"what is the part of this you are least sure about?\" A question with no as an answer gets no.",
  idea: "Ask a question whose easiest answer is not silence, which usually means presupposing that something exists and asking which one rather than whether any do.",
  why: "Does anyone have any concerns is a yes-or-no question addressed to a group, which means it has a free answer available to everybody, and the free answer is the one it gets. It also requires the respondent to classify their unease as a concern, which is a word with weight, and mild reservations do not clear that bar. Between the two, the question filters out precisely the material you wanted, which is the half-formed reservations that have not yet become concerns.\n\nA presupposing question removes both problems. What is the part of this you are least sure about assumes there is one, which is nearly always true, and it asks for a ranking rather than a judgement, which is a much easier cognitive task and carries far less social weight. Nobody is objecting. They are answering a question about relative confidence, and mild answers are legitimate answers rather than failures to have a real concern. The honest caveat is that this is transplanted from survey and interview research rather than tested here, so treat the mechanism as plausible and the improvement as something you should check yourself.",
  failureMode: "You keep asking the old question, get silence, and record agreement. The three problems that surfaced later were all at the level of I was not sure about that bit, which is a thing nobody would ever have volunteered under the label of a concern.",
  worked: "Presuppose, ask for a ranking, and legitimise the small answer:\n\n\"Rather than asking whether anyone has concerns, which I have been doing and which gets me nothing: what is the part of this you are least sure about? Everyone has one, it does not have to be a problem, and I am not going to treat it as an objection. Priya, what is yours?\"\n\nCombining it with going by name is what makes it work. The presupposing question fixes the wording problem and the named turn fixes the free-silence problem, and both need fixing.",
  experiment: "Swap the question for a month and count responses per meeting against your previous baseline. This is one of the few things here you can measure cleanly, because the only thing that changed is nine words.",
  reflection: "What question do you currently ask to solicit objections, and what is its easiest answer?",
  recall: {
    q: "What are the two problems with asking whether anyone has concerns, and what does a presupposing question fix?",
    a: "It is a yes-or-no question to a group, so it has a free answer that costs nobody anything, and it requires classifying unease as a concern, a word with enough weight that mild reservations do not qualify. A presupposing question assumes something exists and asks for a ranking of confidence instead, which is an easier task, carries less social weight, and makes small answers legitimate."
  },
  deepDive: "Here is how I currently ask for objections in meetings. Give me three replacement questions that are hard to answer with silence."
},
{
  id: "receiving-safety-is-not-a-programme",
  track: "receiving", level: "line",
  title: "Psychological safety is a measurement of what happens to people, not a thing you can install",
  source: "Amy Edmondson's psychological safety construct (1999 onwards), and the meta-analytic literature on its correlates",
  gateIntro: "Your organisation has run psychological safety training for all managers. Attendance was ninety per cent and feedback was positive. Six months later the engagement survey question about raising concerns has not moved, and the proposal on the table is a refresher course. You are in the room and you have the survey data in front of you.",
  gatePrompt: "Write the alternative you propose, given that the training was not unreasonable and the people who ran it are present.",
  evidence: "The construct is well established and its associations with learning behaviour, information sharing and performance are supported by meta-analysis. The great majority of that evidence is cross-sectional and self-reported, which limits causal inference badly. Intervention studies are far sparser and their results are inconsistent, so the claim that training managers produces measurable safety is much weaker than the claim that safety correlates with good outcomes.",
  replication: "contested",
  idea: "Treat psychological safety as the observed consequence of what happens to people who speak, and intervene on those consequences rather than on managers' understanding of the concept.",
  why: "The construct is real and the evidence for its correlates is reasonable. What is weak is the step your organisation has taken, which is from a correlational construct to a training intervention, and that step has much less behind it than the construct does. Managers who attended the training now understand the concept. Understanding a concept is not one of the things the evidence associates with outcomes.\n\nThe alternative follows from what safety actually is, which is an expectation people hold about consequences, formed by observing consequences. That means it is measurable in a much more direct way than a survey question: take the last several people who raised something unwelcome and find out what happened to them afterwards. Did the thing change? Were they given work as a result? Did anything happen to their standing, their scope, their next review? That data exists, it is specific, and it points at particular managers and particular incidents. It is also considerably more uncomfortable than a refresher course, which is the honest reason courses keep winning.",
  failureMode: "The refresher runs. Managers learn the concept a second time. The survey question does not move again, and the organisation concludes that psychological safety is difficult to change, when what it has established is that training does not change it.",
  worked: "Concede what the training did well, name the evidential gap without attacking anyone, and propose the harder measurement:\n\n\"I do not think the training was wrong and I am not arguing it was wasted. My concern is that we are measuring understanding and hoping for behaviour.\n\nWhat I would like to do instead is look at consequences. Take the last ten times somebody raised something genuinely unwelcome. What happened to the issue, and what happened to the person? Did they end up owning it, did their scope change, did it show up in a review?\n\nThat is a fortnight of work and it will name specific situations rather than producing a score. I think that is the point. If it turns out nothing bad happens to anyone, the training is not our problem and I will drop this.\"",
  experiment: "Do the consequence audit on your own team first, on the last five objections raised to you. Write down what happened to the issue and to the person in each case. Doing it on yourself before proposing it for others is both fairer and much more persuasive when you propose it.",
  reflection: "What actually happened to the last three people who told you something you did not want to hear?",
  recall: {
    q: "What is well supported about psychological safety and what is not, and what should be measured instead of understanding?",
    a: "Its associations with learning behaviour and performance are supported by meta-analysis, but that evidence is largely cross-sectional and self-reported, and intervention studies are sparse and inconsistent, so training producing safety is much weaker than safety correlating with outcomes. Measure consequences instead: for the last several people who raised something unwelcome, what happened to the issue and to them."
  },
  deepDive: "My company wants to run psychological safety training again. Help me design the consequence audit I would propose instead."
}
);
